  "findings": [
    "A9b is not met: 0.0018 with one torch thread and 0.0023 with two, both above 0.001.",
    "The largest difference is always in a box channel (height or width) at a stride-32 anchor, where values are 415-601 px; it is 6-74 float32 ulps at that value. Class-score channels differ by at most 1.4e-7.",
    "In grid units the box difference is 1e-5 to 8e-5 at every stride; the decoder multiplies by the stride (8, 16, 32; an exact power-of-two multiplication), so the coarsest level produces the largest pixel difference.",
    "Neck features already differ by 6e-6 to 3.6e-5 between ONNX Runtime and PyTorch float32, the same size as the difference between PyTorch float32 and float64 (5e-6 to 3.9e-5): the gap starts as float32 rounding in the convolutions, not at a single operator.",
    "Against a float64 PyTorch reference, ONNX Runtime is within 0.00096 and PyTorch float32 within 0.00188: the export is not less accurate than the PyTorch float32 reference.",
    "PyTorch float32 against itself on the same inputs: one thread vs two threads differs by 0.00189, oneDNN off vs on by 0.00156; both above 0.001. The 0.001 absolute bound on pixel-unit outputs is below the run-to-run variation of the PyTorch reference on this CPU.",
    "The instrumented ONNX graph (extra intermediate outputs) gave a final output identical to the original in all four inputs."
  ],
  "what_was_not_done": "No change to the criterion, weights, export settings, thresholds or service (still PyTorch). No training, no image evaluation; task-level agreement is the earlier evidence (test mAP 0.983/0.852 ONNX vs 0.984/0.850 PyTorch; 50 real images: same detections and classes, final boxes within 0.00027 px).",
  "context_sources": [
    {
      "url": "https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/_sources/advanced/super_resolution_with_onnxruntime.rst.txt",
